2. If it does, sanitize the path to prevent it from being interpreted as a protocol-relative URL. This is done by replacing the leading slashes with `/%2F`, which is the URL encoding for `/`. The implementation will be `new_path = '/%2F' + new_path.lstrip('/')`. This robustly handles any number of leading slashes.

Function: vul_py_10_2
1. Locate the sanitization logic within the `if url.startswith('//'):` block.
2. Correct the format string from `'/%%2F%s'` to `'/%2F%s'` to ensure the prepended slash is properly URL-encoded.
3. Replace the slice `url[2:]` with the method `url.lstrip('/')`. This ensures that all leading slashes are removed before prepending `/%2F`, correctly handling cases with more than two leading slashes.

